---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The Other Emigration"
  eyebrow="Two American diasporas this archive did not know it had"
  dek="There is a page here about Port Pirie because the family knew about Port Pirie. Nobody knew about Youngstown, Johnstown, Brooklyn, Leadville, Calumet or Chicago — and the records are not scarce. They were simply never looked for.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Everything below was found in a single day on a commercial index, during a
    two-day trial. <strong>None of it is a claim of descent.</strong> It is a record of where people
    carrying these surnames went, what they gave as the village they had left, and who they named as
    kin — because that last field is what will eventually separate ours from not-ours.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The Žubrinići went twice, and the spelling tells you which</h2>
    <p class="lede">Search <span class="mono">Zubrinich</span> and you get <strong>191 records</strong>,
    almost all <a href={u("/port-pirie/")}>Australian</a>. Search <span class="mono">Zubrinic</span> —
    the unanglicised form — and you get a <strong>different 119</strong>, almost all American.
    <strong>Anybody searching one spelling has been missing the other entirely.</strong></p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Under <span class="mono">Zubrinich</span> — America</h3>
        <p>A cluster at <strong>Youngstown, Ohio</strong> and <strong>Johnstown,
        Pennsylvania</strong> — Steve, George, Anton, Pete, Daniel, Milan, Anastasia — with WWI
        draft cards, censuses from 1905, a Minnesota state census, a Canadian border crossing at
        Vermont, and an <strong>Ivan Zubrinich born 1888 living in Brooklyn</strong> in 1930.</p>
        <p class="dek">For a surname carried by roughly <strong>312 people on earth</strong>, a
        second emigration is not a coincidence. It is also not yet a connection.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Under <span class="mono">Zubrinic</span> — Lika, named</h3>
        <p>These manifests give the <strong>village left behind</strong>:</p>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li><strong>Šumećica</strong> — Franjo, 1910, leaving a wife Franci and a brother Ture</li>
          <li><strong>Otočac</strong> — Georg 1903, “Mjos” 1907 (sailing from <strong>Fiume</strong>)</li>
          <li><strong>Prozor</strong> — Eva 1907, Miko 1910, Nikola 1911, Anton 1911,
            <a href={u("/people/matthaus-mate-zubrinic/")}>Mate</a> 1911, Miho 1912</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="dek">Šumećica houses 2 and 24, and Prozor 46 and 50, are the oldest addresses in
        this archive. <a href={u("/frontier/")}>The Frontier →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Prozor is emptying, and this archive stops just before it happens</h2>
    <p class="lede">Six people leave one village in five years — and the archive's own record of
    that village ends before any of them were born.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Born</th><th>Sailed</th><th>Father named on the manifest</th><th>Kin met or left</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Eva Zubrinic</td><td class="mono">1880</td><td class="mono">1907</td><td class="dim">—</td><td class="dim">bro-in-law <strong>Joso Gomerčić</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Miko Zubrinic</td><td class="mono">1893</td><td class="mono">1910</td><td><strong>Ivan Zubrinic</strong></td><td class="dim">uncle <strong>Josef Kostelac</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nikola Zubrinic</td><td class="mono">1886</td><td class="mono">1911</td><td><strong>Sandro Zubrinic</strong></td><td class="dim">bro-in-law <strong>Jus Orešković</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anton Zubrinic</td><td class="mono">1891</td><td class="mono">1911</td><td><strong>Marco Zubrinic</strong></td><td class="dim">—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mate Žubrinić</strong></td><td class="mono">1867</td><td class="mono">1911</td><td class="dim">Michael, per this archive</td><td class="dim">wife Roza · nephew <strong>Stipan Kostelac</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Miho Zubrinic</td><td class="dim">—</td><td class="mono">1912</td><td class="dim">—</td><td class="dim">bro-in-law Nikola Ladisić</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>The gap</h3>
      <p>This archive documents <strong>Prozor 50</strong> in detail — Michael Žubrinić, born 1826,
      and his seven children, Nikola 1858 through Franjo 1874. <strong>And then it stops. There is
      not one Žubrinić born at Prozor between 1875 and 1900 anywhere on this site.</strong></p>
      <p>The manifests document precisely that missing generation, and name its fathers:
      <strong>Ivan</strong>, <strong>Sandro</strong> and <strong>Marco</strong>. Ivan is almost
      certainly <a href={u("/people/ioaness-ivan-zubrinic/")}>Ivan of Prozor 50, born 1870</a>, whose
      own record also stops at his birth. <strong>Sandro and Marco are not here at all</strong> in
      any generation that could father a child in the 1880s — so Prozor held at least two more
      Žubrinić households than this archive knows of.</p>
      <p><strong>And the in-laws are people we already hold.</strong> <a href={u("/connected/")}>Kostelac</a>
      twice, <strong>Orešković</strong> — fourteen of them here — and <strong>Gomerčić</strong>.
      The Prozor emigrants married into the same families as the Prozor people already in this
      archive. Not proof of kinship. A very strong hint of it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the Kalanji went to the mining towns</h2>
    <p class="lede">Eighty-one migration records for a family this archive holds 216 people of — and
    <strong>it records no Kalanj emigration at all</strong>. They sail 1891–1914, peaking in 1907,
    1909 and 1913, into <strong>Leadville, Colorado</strong>; <strong>Calumet and Houghton,
    Michigan</strong>; <strong>Highland Boy, Utah</strong>; Duluth, Chicago, Akron, Reno; and
    <strong>British Columbia</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>The caution comes before the finding.</strong> Split the given names and they fall in
    two: <em>Ivan, Joso, Mate, Mile, Miko, Luka, Ana, Kata, Marica</em> — and <em>Božo, Đuro,
    Stevan, Sava, Todora, Milica, Jovan</em>. The second column is not the naming pool of Catholic
    Klenovica. <strong>Kalanj is also a Serbian surname in Lika</strong>, and the Krajina sent its
    own emigration to exactly these towns in exactly these years.</p>
    <p>Reading seven of the manifests gave four different home villages — <strong>Brinje,
    Plaščica, Kurjak, Velgovica</strong> — none within forty kilometres of another. <strong>Kalanj
    is a regional surname, not one household.</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Of which exactly one matters.</strong> <a href={u("/gazetteer/")}>Brinje</a> is the
    parish this archive's own 1864 baptism names for the Klenovica Kalanji — and following that
    single word inland is what corrected the family's origin. <a href={u("/kalanj/")}>Kalanj →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this is worth, and what it is not</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>One person has actually been placed.</strong>
      <a href={u("/people/matthaus-mate-zubrinic/")}>Mate Žubrinić of Prozor 50</a>, born 29 December
      1867, sailed to New York in 1911 aged 43. His record here previously stopped at his birth.</li>
      <li><strong>One household is fully documented and belongs to nobody here.</strong>
      <strong>Ivan Zubrinic, born 30 June 1888 at Otočac</strong> — sworn on a Brooklyn
      naturalisation — who sailed to Baltimore in 1907, whose wife Kata and sons Juraj and Josip
      followed in 1932, and who died in New York in 1963. This archive holds an Ivan born
      <em>16 August 1887</em> at Otočac and a Stjepan born 1888. <strong>He is neither.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Nothing has been added to the tree.</strong> Every name above is recorded as what
      a document says, not as a relative. <a href={u("/method/")}>Method →</a></li>
      <li><strong>And the ask has changed because of it.</strong> The
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>request to Gospić</a> asked for marriages across a district. It now
      asks for the <strong>Otočac baptisms for Prozor, 1875–1895</strong> — one volume, one village,
      twenty years, and a whole generation restored.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a class="btn" href={u("/port-pirie/")}>Port Pirie →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/zubrinic-roots/")}>The Žubrinić fragments →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/work/")}>What they did →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
